{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000045","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"To implement substitution in a graph, unused variables require disposal and repeated variables require explicit sharing.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects the variable-occurrence cases to graph machinery."}]},{"targetId":"n0000048","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The binding port identifies where occurrences of the parameter connect, while the body port preserves the expression evaluated after binding.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the separate roles of the two lambda ports."}]},{"targetId":"n0000069","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Copying an unreduced argument duplicates its remaining computation, whereas sharing can let one reduction update all uses.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"supports","reason":"States why duplicated redexes cause repeated work."}]},{"targetId":"n0000080","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The four subsystems distinguish which variable-use disciplines permit duplication, erasure, or both.","densityScore":0.73,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Supplies the purpose of the subsystem classification."}]},{"targetId":"n0000084","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Sequential reduction chooses one occurrence at a time, so it cannot always preserve a computation that several occurrences should observe jointly.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the limitation in operational terms."}]},{"targetId":"n0000087","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Reducing before copying avoids evaluating the same shared redex independently in each future copy.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Justifies reduce-once sharing."}]},{"targetId":"n0000091","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A rewrite is triggered when two agents meet at their principal ports, making the redex boundary local to that pair.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Adds the condition that makes local interaction possible."}]},{"targetId":"n0000109","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Because the rule changes only the active pair and its incident wires, unrelated parts of the graph need not be inspected or rewritten.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Derives locality from the reconnection rule."}]},{"targetId":"n0000135","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An interaction step is the atomic unit of reduction: one active pair is replaced according to its local rule.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"fills","reason":"Makes the step boundary explicit."}]},{"targetId":"n0000138","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"An agent cannot belong to two active pairs because it has only one principal port.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"supports","reason":"States the structural reason independent pairs are disjoint."}]},{"targetId":"n0000140","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The one-step diamond property requires two competing one-step reductions to be joinable by one further step on each branch.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"fills","reason":"Formalizes the diagram's confluence condition."}]},{"targetId":"n0000142","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The two branches represent alternative choices from the same net, and their convergence represents agreement of those choices.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains what the diagram establishes."}]},{"targetId":"n0000143","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The diagram is stronger than eventual convergence because it fixes the joining distance to one interaction per branch.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Highlights the significance of the one-step diamond."}]},{"targetId":"n0000144","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Disjoint active pairs commute because neither rewrite changes an agent or wire endpoint owned by the other pair.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why execution order does not matter."}]},{"targetId":"n0000147","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Confluence concerns agreement among reduction paths; normalization concerns whether any path reaches a normal form at all.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"fills","reason":"Separates the two properties."}]},{"targetId":"n0000149","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Scope delimiters make sharing contexts explicit by forcing extra agents to travel with or cross context boundaries.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why bookkeeping agents create overhead."}]},{"targetId":"n0000152","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Encoding context on replicator ports removes the need to represent that context as a separate chain of delimiter agents.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects the design to the preceding critique."}]},{"targetId":"n0000155","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"A net's agent type determines which local interaction rule applies when its principal port meets another principal port.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"enables","reason":"Links agent vocabulary to reduction rules."}]},{"targetId":"n0000162","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The drawings encode port identity by position, so rotating an agent can change which visible side represents a given auxiliary-port index.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Prepares readers to distinguish geometry from port identity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000165","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The two fan auxiliaries carry the two ordered connections needed by application or abstraction, while the principal port exposes the construct to interaction.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why a fan has this arity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000168","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"When erasure reaches a subgraph, local deletion rules propagate the fact that no result from that subgraph is needed.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects an unused binder to graph-wide disposal."}]},{"targetId":"n0000171","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Replicator arity is the number of distinct uses currently joined to the shared value.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the operational meaning of arity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000174","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Deltas make context adjustment local: a replica can determine its next level from the exit port it traverses.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the purpose of per-port offsets."}]},{"targetId":"n0000181","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The agent inventory can be read as a cost model: omission needs erasure, repeated use needs replication, and term shape needs fans.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"supports","reason":"Makes the taxonomy-agent correspondence useful."}]},{"targetId":"n0000183","injections":[{"type":"uncertainty","text":"Using level equality as a complete equality test is valid only under the stated translation invariant, not for arbitrary nets.","densityScore":0.93,"relation":"supports","reason":"Preserves the boundary on the implementation shortcut."}]},{"targetId":"n0000185","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Orientation describes the direction of a connection relative to the chosen root, independently of the graph's undirected wire drawing.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"Clarifies parent and child terminology."}]},{"targetId":"n0000188","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The three families classify an active pair by whether it removes structure, propagates non-use, or distributes structure across surrounding ports.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the organizing principle."}]},{"targetId":"n0000190","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Cancellation preserves the surrounding graph by pairing corresponding auxiliary ports after removing the two equal agents.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the reconnection invariant."}]},{"targetId":"n0000192","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Since an eraser offers no auxiliary continuation, interaction with it cannot preserve any branch through the erased neighbour.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why deletion follows from zero arity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000194","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Passage-with-copying exchanges the agents' surrounding contexts: each outgoing auxiliary port requires a corresponding local copy.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the dependence on arity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000198","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Erasure propagates instead of stopping at one agent because every remaining branch that could demand the value must receive its own eraser.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why zero-copy deletion still creates downstream erasers."}]},{"targetId":"n0000200","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Fan annihilation implements substitution structurally by reconnecting the application result to the body and the argument to the bound-variable connection.","densityScore":0.92,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects the local rule to beta reduction."}]},{"targetId":"n0000201","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The diagram identifies the two ordered auxiliary connections that must be preserved when the application and abstraction fans annihilate.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains what to inspect in the pre-reduction graph."}]},{"targetId":"n0000203","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"After annihilation, the result wire connects to the lambda body and the argument wire connects to the variable-use connection.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"fills","reason":"States the post-reduction reconnection directly."}]},{"targetId":"n0000204","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Port order is semantic, while left and right are drawing-dependent; the crossing follows from preserving port order across rotation.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"supports","reason":"Distinguishes structural identity from layout."}]},{"targetId":"n0000206","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The fan-replicator interaction forms a rectangular family of connections: one copy along each auxiliary direction of each participating agent.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Summarizes the bidirectional copying pattern."}]},{"targetId":"n0000208","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"This is an active pair between a three-port replicator and a two-port fan, so the resulting rule must account for both arities.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"enables","reason":"Sets up the expected copy counts."}]},{"targetId":"n0000210","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The result has three fan copies because the replicator has three auxiliaries, and two replicator copies because the fan has two.","densityScore":0.89,"relation":"fills","reason":"Explains the diagram's 3-by-2 structure."}]},{"targetId":"n0000211","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The formula preserves port identities across copying, so every new connection is determined even when the diagram's geometry is rotated.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why the indexed rule is unambiguous."}]},{"targetId":"n0000213","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The copy counts follow independently from the source agent arities, preventing a visual reading of the diagram from hiding missing copies.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"supports","reason":"Reinforces the counting invariant."}]},{"targetId":"n0000215","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"Equality determines the cancellation branch; only unequal replicators enter the copying-and-level-adjustment branch.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"enables","reason":"Makes the rule decision explicit."}]},{"targetId":"n0000216","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"In the unequal case, the lower-level replicator's port deltas are applied to the copies of the higher-level replicator that exit through those ports.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies which agent receives level changes."}]},{"targetId":"n0000218","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"This diagram is the decision point for the equal case: both replicators have the same displayed level and matching port structure.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"enables","reason":"Connects the picture to cancellation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000221","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Annihilation reconnects equal-indexed ports, so apparent crossing is a consequence of the lower agent's rotated port order.","densityScore":0.89,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the wire geometry after cancellation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000223","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The unequal levels select the commuting rule rather than cancellation, with R's deltas determining the levels of the H copies.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"enables","reason":"Sets up the next diagram's numeric outcome."}]},{"targetId":"n0000225","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Each top H copy corresponds to one auxiliary port of R, while each bottom R copy corresponds to one auxiliary port of H.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"fills","reason":"Explains the two copy families in the result."}]},{"targetId":"n0000226","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Only H's copies change level because they traverse R's delta-labelled exits; copying R through H does not apply an R exit delta to R itself.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"supports","reason":"Justifies the asymmetric level update."}]},{"targetId":"n0000227","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The complete grid records all pairwise auxiliary connections created by commuting the two agents, not additional independent interactions.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Prevents interpreting the grid as unrelated work."}]}]}
